      id: "cv-etudiant",
      title: "Creating an Impactful Resume as a Student",
      excerpt: "No experience? No panic. Here is how to turn your school projects into major assets.",
      content: `<h2>The Challenge of a Resume without Experience</h2><p>Writing a resume when you are a student or recent graduate can feel like a real headache. How do you fill a whole page when you've never worked? The key lies in a shift of perspective: professional experience is not the only form of experience that has value in the eyes of a recruiter.</p><h2>1. Pimp Your Education Section</h2><p>Since you have little professional experience, your education is your greatest asset. Do not just list the name of your school. Detail it:</p><ul><li><strong>Relevant subjects:</strong> List 3 or 4 major courses that are directly related to the targeted internship.</li><li><strong>Honors and rankings:</strong> If you were in the top 10% of your class, mention it!</li><li><strong>International experience:</strong> Any university exchange (Erasmus) or language stay shows your open-mindedness and adaptability.</li></ul><h2>2. Turn Academic Projects into Real Experiences</h2><p>Did you complete a final year project? A thesis? A group case study lasting several months? Treat these academic projects exactly like jobs. Use the same structure: <em>Project Title, Role Played, Actions Taken, Results Achieved.</em></p><h2>3. The Power of Volunteering and Student Associations</h2><p>Volunteering is unpaid professional experience. Having been treasurer of the Student Union proves you know how to manage a budget. Organizing a gala proves your event management and stress management skills.</p><h2>FAQ: Students and Recent Graduates</h2><h3>Should I include my summer jobs (fast food, babysitting)?</h3><p>Absolutely! These jobs, even if unrelated to your future profession, prove your punctuality, customer service skills, and ability to work in a team under pressure.</p><h3>Should I put a title on my resume?</h3><p>Yes, the title is crucial. It indicates your immediate objective. Instead of \"3rd year student\", write \"Seeking Internship (6 months) - Digital Marketing Assistant - Starting Jan 2027\".</p>`,
      date: "2026-08-07",
      category: "Beginner",
      readTime: "10 min",
      image: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1523240795612-9a054b0db644?q=80&w=800&auto=format&fit=crop"
